```python
def count_primes(n):
    if n < 2:
        return 0
    is_prime = [True] * (n + 1)
    is_prime[0] = is_prime[1] = False
    p = 2
    while p * p <= n:
        if is_prime[p]:
            is_prime[p * p :: p] = [False] * len(range(p * p, n + 1, p))
        p += 1
    return sum(is_prime)
```

The sieve runs in O(n log log n). Starting at `p * p` works because smaller multiples were already crossed out by smaller primes, and slice assignment crosses out a whole series at once.

